1️⃣ Infix → Postfix
Main rule

When an operator comes from input, compare it with the operator at the TOP of STACK.

📦 Comparison Box — POSTFIX

| Stack TOP       | Incoming Operator | What to do                  |
| --------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------- |
| `+` / `-`       | `*` / `/` / `%`   | **PUSH** incoming           |
| `+` / `-`       | `+` / `-`         | **POP**, then compare again |
| `+` / `-`       | `^`               | **PUSH** incoming           |
| `*` / `/` / `%` | `+` / `-`         | **POP** stack               |
| `*` / `/` / `%` | `*` / `/` / `%`   | **POP** stack               |
| `*` / `/` / `%` | `^`               | **PUSH** incoming           |
| `^`             | `+` / `-`         | **POP** stack               |
| `^`             | `*` / `/` / `%`   | **POP** stack               |
| `^`             | `^`               | **PUSH** incoming*          |



2️⃣ Infix → Prefix

Here you first:

Step 1

Reverse the infix expression

Step 2

Swap ( and )

Step 3

Perform the operator conversion.

📦 Comparison Box — PREFIX
Stack TOP	Incoming Operator	What to do
+ / -	* / / / %	PUSH incoming
+ / -	+ / -	PUSH incoming*
+ / -	^	PUSH incoming
* / / / %	+ / -	POP stack
* / / / %	* / / / %	PUSH incoming*
* / / / %	^	PUSH incoming
^	+ / -	POP stack
^	* / / / %	POP stack
^	^	POP stack*

The * cases are the important difference from the normal postfix conversion.



Incoming has HIGHER priority
        ↓
      PUSH

Incoming has LOWER priority
        ↓
       POP

Same priority
        ↓
       POP